{
 "id": "2022-zhu-garden",
 "name": "Garden Depth Perception: Magnitude Estimation",
 "description": "Method replication: participants look out over the pond of the Liu Garden, Suzhou, from the Lvyin Pavilion in panoramic photographs and estimate the garden's depth (distance to its far end) by magnitude estimation. The middle-position panorama A2 is the standard (depth 100); each round shows it for 45 s, then a comparative panorama for 45 s, which the participant gives a number relative to 100, followed by 15 s with eyes closed. Each participant is assigned one of five sessions: A1 and A3 (left and right viewpoints) plus the three panoramas of one depth-cue treatment (frame shape, lotus occlusion, pavilion interior, waterside buildings or lotus texture), as in Task 1 of Zhu, Gu, Ohno & Kong (2022), Frontiers in Psychology 13: 963600. The 18 panoramas are those printed in its Figure 1 (CC BY 4.0); the paper's mean estimates are stored for comparison. Differences from the original study: (1) the panoramas are shown on an ordinary screen instead of a three-wall CAVE, at the size printed in the paper (about 1000 px wide), with the panel letters removed; (2) sessions are assigned at random online, not in a lab; (3) the paper's practice trial and Task 2 (adjusting a 3D model wall) are not included; (4) the question about the viewing device is added.",
 "author": "Zhu, Gu, Ohno & Kong",
 "year": "2022",
 "category": "Academic Research",
